    "q-54238": {
      "dom_id": "step-q54238",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14203245/assets/32938bfdcb2ed9130304722b408b304802062b701bb35c68d7d3333c7cfe8ee6.png)\n\nGiven the triangle above, find the length of $\\overset{―}{AB}$ rounded to one decimal place.",
      "worked_solution": "For an angle $\\theta $ in a right triangle, the trigonometric ratios are as follows:\n\n- $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}}$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the length of the hypotenuse $\\overset{―}{AC}$ and wish to find the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{AB}$ opposite the given angle $∠C.$\n\nThe trigonometric ratio that relates the opposite leg to the hypotenuse is *sine*. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(m∠C) & =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡({52}^{∘}) & =\\frac{AB}{15} \\\\ AB & =15\\operatorname{sin}⁡({52}^{∘}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator to evaluate the trigonometric ratio, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =15\\operatorname{sin}⁡({52}^{∘}) \\\\  & =15(0.788\\,01…) \\\\  & =11.820\\,16… \\\\  & ≈11.8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
