    "q-122362": {
      "dom_id": "step-q122362",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14203245/assets/804c19a20b6f47707a07c7bdcee3cbd727eec2c270109e30eb55d949ace8edf0.png)\n\nCalculate the value of $BC$ to the nearest integer.",
      "worked_solution": "For an angle $\\theta $ in a right triangle, the trigonometric ratios are as follows:\n\n- $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}}$\n\nNotice that we are given the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{AC}$ opposite to the given angle $∠B$ and wish to find the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{BC}$ adjacent $∠B.$\n\nThe trigonometric ratio that relates the opposite leg to the adjacent leg is *tangent*. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠B) & =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({20}^{∘}) & =\\frac{2.91}{BC} \\\\ BC\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡({20}^{∘}) & =2.91 \\\\ BC & =\\frac{2.91}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡({20}^{∘})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator to evaluate the trigonometric ratio, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =\\frac{2.91}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡({20}^{∘})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2.91}{(0.363\\,97…)} \\\\  & =7.995\\,15… \\\\  & ≈8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.",
