    "q-122357": {
      "dom_id": "step-q122357",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14203245/assets/a9c863e0cdb9788e06c527e17207e87ba2281ab21c9bd0836009ca518a15631a.png)\n\nGiven the triangle above, find the length of $\\overset{―}{AB}$ rounded to one decimal place.",
      "worked_solution": "For an angle $\\theta $ in a right triangle, the trigonometric ratios are as follows:\n\n- $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}}$\n\nNotice that we are given the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{BC}$ adjacent to the given angle $∠C$ and wish to find the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{AB}$ opposite $∠C.$\n\nThe trigonometric ratio that relates the opposite leg to the adjacent leg is *tangent*. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠C) & =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({56}^{∘}) & =\\frac{AB}{9} \\\\ AB & =9\\operatorname{tan}⁡({56}^{∘}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator to evaluate the trigonometric ratio, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =9\\operatorname{tan}⁡({56}^{∘}) \\\\  & =9(1.482\\,56…) \\\\  & =13.343\\,04… \\\\  & ≈13.3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.",
